ply":"2025-09-08T12:16:33.103478Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"/kaggle/input/glovetwitter27b100dtxt/glove.twitter.27B.100d.txt\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":1},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Semantic Early Warning System (SEWS) using Twitter GloVe Embeddings\n\n## Project Overview\nThis project builds a **real-world, enterprise-grade system** to detect **harmful narratives and disinformation campaigns** on social media using **GloVe word embeddings** (trained on Twitter data).  \nRather than just classifying tweets, our system aims to provide **early detection of coordinated disinformation** by analyzing semantic patterns, anomalies, and topic drifts.\n\n---\n\n## End-to-End Pipeline\n\n### 1. **Introduction & Problem Context**\n- Why embeddings matter in real-world AI.\n- The societal problem: disinformation, hate speech, and emerging harmful narratives.\n\n### 2. **Data Loading & Exploration**\n- Load `glove.twitter.27B.100d.txt` (100-dimensional embeddings).\n- Explore vocabulary size, embedding norms, and similarity relationships.\n\n### 3. **Embedding Visualization**\n- Use **PCA/UMAP/t-SNE** to project embeddings into 2D.\n- Visualize clusters of semantically similar words (e.g., medical, political, harmful).\n\n### 4. **Sentence/Tweet Representation**\n- Build tweet embeddings using tokenized words + GloVe vectors.\n- Compare approaches: simple averaging, TF-IDF weighting, and contextual models.\n\n### 5. **Feature Engineering**\n- Cluster words/tweets into semantic groups.\n- Track **temporal shifts** in language (drift detection).\n- Extract anomalies in semantic space using unsupervised methods.\n\n### 6. **Model Development**\n- **Supervised Model**: Train classifier (e.g., LightGBM/XGBoost) for labeled disinformation/hate datasets.\n- **Unsupervised Model**: Isolation Forest / Autoencoder to detect emerging harmful narratives.\n- Ensemble both approaches for robustness.\n\n### 7. **Interpretability & Fairness**\n- Use **SHAP/LIME** for explanation of predictions.\n- Perform **bias checks** and apply debiasing techniques to embeddings.\n\n### 8. **Deployment Strategy**\n- Build a microservice with REST API for live detection.\n- Provide dashboards for visualization and interpretability.\n- Ensure compliance with **GDPR/AI Act** through transparency and auditability.\n\n### 9. **Monitoring & Maintenance**\n- Detect embedding drift as language evolves (new memes, slang, political shifts).\n- Schedule periodic retraining.\n- Continuous bias audits.\n\n---\n\n##  Impact\nBy the end of this notebook, we will have a prototype of the **Semantic Early Warning System (SEWS)**—a tool to help governments, NGOs, and platforms **spot dangerous narratives before they spread**. This isn’t just about prediction—it’s about **building trust, safety, and resilience in digital communities**.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Environment Setup & Library Imports\n\n# Core data science\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Visualization\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\n# Dimensionality reduction & clustering\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nfrom sklearn.manifold import TSNE\nfrom sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import IsolationForest\n\n# Deep learning (GPU-enabled)\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.optim as optim\nfrom torch.utils.data import Dataset, DataLoader\n\n# Interpretability\nimport shap\n\n# System & utilities\nimport os\nimport gc\nimport random\n\n# Check GPU availability\nif torch.cuda.is_available():\n    device = torch.device(\"cuda\")\n    print(\" GPU is available. Using:\", torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))\nelse:\n    device = torch.device(\"cpu\")\n    print(\" GPU not found. Using CPU instead.\")\n\n# Set global styles\nsns.set(style=\"whitegrid\", palette=\"muted\", font_scale=1.2)\n\n# Reproducibility\nSEED = 42\nrandom.seed(SEED)\nnp.random.seed(SEED)\ntorch.manual_seed(SEED)\nif torch.cuda.is_available():\n    torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(SEED)\n    ","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-08T12:20:22.139038Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-08T12:20:22.139888Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-08T12:20:29.788662Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-09-08T12:20:22.139852Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-08T12:20:29.787951Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":" GPU is available. Using: Tesla P100-PCIE-16GB\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":2},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Load GloVe Twitter 27B 100d embeddings\n\nGLOVE_PATH = \"/kaggle/input/glovetwitter27b100dtxt/glove.twitter.27B.100d.txt\"\n\nembeddings_index = {}\nwith open(GLOVE_PATH, encoding=\"utf8\") as f:\n    for line in f:\n        values = line.strip().split()\n        word = values[0]\n        vector = np.asarray(values[1:], dtype=\"float32\")\n        embeddings_index[word] = vector\n\nprint(\" Loaded GloVe embeddings\")\nprint(\"Vocabulary size:\", len(embeddings_index))\nprint(\"Vector dimension:\", len(next(iter(embeddings_index.values()))))\n\n# Quick check of a few embeddings\nfor w in [\"love\", \"hate\", \"virus\", \"freedom\"]:\n    if w in embeddings_index:\n        print(f\"{w}: {embeddings_index[w][:10]}...\")  # print first 10 dims\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-08T12:21:34.209771Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-08T12:21:34.210248Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-08T12:22:05.518700Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-09-08T12:21:34.210226Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-08T12:22:05.517847Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":" Loaded GloVe embeddings\nVocabulary size: 1193514\nVector dimension: 100\nlove: [-0.48371  -0.27318  -0.04684   0.70274  -0.56301  -0.17214   0.81775\n -0.066641  0.020224 -0.23878 ]...\nhate: [-0.089292  0.025274  0.34284   0.7628   -0.57351   0.161     0.26045\n  0.23925   0.15588   0.83302 ]...\nvirus: [-0.091096  0.2778   -1.2006    0.53803   0.69579  -0.44918  -0.1778